text:
alex: Heads-up — found a reproducible telemetry bug in the Node sampleapp during onboarding. Debug-level logs are losing the x-request-id after an auth-retry path. Reproducer attached below, short logs and a snippet.

maria: ugh that sounds bad for tracing. Is it only Node?

taylor: might be related to our OpenTelemetry http hook ordering + the fetch-retry polyfill we recommend in the quickstart. seen similar where header gets rewritten on retry.

alex: repro steps: 1) start sampleapp with NODE_ENV=dev and DEBUG=sampleapp* 2) enable fetch-retry with maxRetries=1 3) trigger /generate route. Logs show request_id initially set, then disappears after the retry.

alex: minimal repro (Node):
```js
// sample-repro.js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fetchRetry = require('fetch-retry')(fetch);
// otel auto-instrumentation hooks loaded before app start
async function call() {
  const res = await fetchRetry('https://api.internal/generate', {
    retries: 1,
    retryOn: [502],
    headers: { 'x-request-id': 'req-1234' }
  });
  console.log(await res.text());
}
call();
```

kevin: can you paste the raw logs? curious if otel is redacting the header or if the header gets dropped before otel sees it.

alex: raw logs (trimmed):
```
2024-09-18T12:01:02.123Z sampleapp INFO incoming req id=req-1234 route=/generate
2024-09-18T12:01:02.456Z sampleapp DEBUG http.outgoing host=api.internal x-request-id=req-1234
2024-09-18T12:01:02.789Z sampleapp WARN http.retry 502, retrying...
2024-09-18T12:01:03.012Z sampleapp DEBUG http.outgoing host=api.internal x-request-id=-
2024-09-18T12:01:03.345Z sampleapp INFO request complete route=/generate req_id=-
```

maria: looks like the second outgoing call lost the header entirely. Not redaction (we'd still have masked value). Possibly fetch-retry re-creates the request object and our OTEL hook doesn't re-attach baggage/headers on clone.

taylor: yes. Our Node Otel hook attaches request attributes when instrumentation sees the outgoing span. If the library clones the Request and we instrument earlier, the header won't get copied into the new Request object.

kevin: mitigation options: 1) Teach the SDK to preserve x-request-id in the request context and re-inject on retries. 2) Update quickstart to recommend a different retry pattern. 3) Patch fetch-retry or add a small wrapper in sampleapp.

alex: patch idea I prototyped: small middleware that reads req.id from incoming express context and stores in AsyncLocalStorage; our otel hook then always injects that header when creating outgoing calls. Simple and works in repro.

alex: middleware snippet:
```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-request-id'] || generateId();
  asyncLocalStorage.run({ requestId: id }, () => next());
});
```
and in otel http hook inject: headers['x-request-id'] = asyncLocalStorage.getStore()?.requestId || headers['x-request-id'];

maria: nice. That keeps semantics consistent across retries. Does this break any redaction rules? we need to ensure we don't re-inject sensitive tokens.

taylor: good point — only propagate id-like headers (x-request-id, traceparent, baggage). We should avoid touching Authorization. Add a allowlist filter.

kevin: can we add a unit/integration test in sampleapp e2e suite that simulates a retry and asserts x-request-id preserved? Then we can mark this as a regression test.

alex: will add test. One more oddity: when DEBUG=sampleapp:telemetry the log level shows masked IDs in dev but not in prod. Looks like our redaction hook runs at logger formatter stage and masks if NODE_ENV!=dev. So reproducer had '-' only because redaction saw empty header; we need to be careful not to mask an actual removed value.

maria: TL;DR action items: @taylor update otel hook to re-inject from ALS allowlist; @alex add middleware + e2e test; @kevin update quickstart notes for the time being to call out fetch-retry incompatibility. I'll open an issue and link PRs. :thumbsup:

telemetry-bot: Created issue #4312: "Node sampleapp: x-request-id lost across fetch-retry & OpenTelemetry hook ordering" (assigned to devex).

kevin: thanks. Also flagging for SDK parity - we should run the same repro against Go and Python sampleapps to confirm they don't have the same gap. If they do, we need a cross-SDK pattern.

alex: agreed. I'll run quick checks against Go sampleapp tomorrow and post results here.
